# 🧠 Quick Mental Model

When working with SFTP, remember this:

```text
                    SFTP
                     │
          ┌──────────┴──────────┐
          │                     │
       REMOTE                  LOCAL
       AWS EC2                 Your PC
          │                     │
     ls / cd / pwd        lls / lcd / lpwd
          │                     │
          └──────────┬──────────┘
                     │
                File Transfer
                     │
             ┌───────┴───────┐
             │               │
            put             get
             │               │
        Local → Remote   Remote → Local
```

### The commands to remember

```text
ls       → Remote files
lls      → Local files

cd       → Remote directory
lcd      → Local directory

pwd      → Remote location
lpwd     → Local location

put      → Upload
get      → Download

put -r   → Upload directory
get -r   → Download directory

!        → Run local shell command

exit     → Leave SFTP
```

---

# 🎯 Key Takeaways

By the end of this lesson, you should understand:

- **SFTP creates an interactive file-transfer session.**
- You work with both a **local machine and a remote machine**.
- Commands such as `ls`, `cd`, and `pwd` operate on the **remote server**.
- Commands such as `lls`, `lcd`, and `lpwd` operate on the **local machine**.
- `put` transfers files **Local → Remote**.
- `get` transfers files **Remote → Local**.
- `-r` allows directories to be transferred recursively.
- `!` lets you execute local shell commands without leaving SFTP.
- Directory access requires the appropriate Linux permissions, including the **execute (`x`) bit**.

> 💡 **The most important thing to remember:**  
> **SFTP is not just a file-transfer command — it is an interactive session where you manage both sides of the connection.**
